I have no idea what you mean. "show feedback"? Can you be more
specific, please?


> 2. I would like user to click the jar file to run my application
> without type "R CMD" in terminal window.

You can't, a simple JAR won't do. You have to use a launcher one way
or another. Practically, you have two options:

a) use JRI bootstrap - that allows you to create a special JAR file
that contains the library and your JAR file. The bootstrap code
unpacks the library, sets the environment up and then starts your JAR
file (see the bootstrap directory in the JRI sources). Although it's
quite neat, I didn't have time to document it well, so let me know if
you like the idea but you get stuck in the middle.

b) use a native launcher (e.g. a shell script or an executable like
JGR does). If you program is an R package, look at the JGR launcher
sources. Otherwise it's up to you how you store the JAR within (e.g.
on a Mac it's easy, because you can put it in the application bundle;
on Windows you could append it on the executable itself ...).


> I used this command to set R_HOME: set env R_HOME /Library/
> Frameworks/R.framework/Resources/R and it didn't work. I still get
> the same error message.
>

Depending on the shell (default is  bash) it should be
export R_HOME=/Library/Frameworks/R.framework/Resources
java -jar MyApp.jar
but setting the env var for LS app doesn't work, so the user still
can't double-click on it. Again, you need a launcher.


> 3. I recompiled the JRI. Can you see anything wrong from the below
> output?
>

Nope.

Cheers,
Simon


